{
  "master_id": "bill-carter",
  "work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
  "usage_notes": [
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "harmonic-family-membership",
      "narrative": "In any major key, exactly two maj7 chords are diatonic: the I and the IV. Carter states: \"Two major sevenths I and IV\" (Ch12 p.117). This membership defines where maj7 naturally appears without alteration or borrowing in the diatonic seventh-chord taxonomy.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 12 p.117"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "open-position-voicing",
      "narrative": "For physical playability on guitar, the third of the maj7 chord is displaced up an octave from its close-position location. Carter explains: \"the third of the chord has been moved an octave higher\" as a \"standard alternate configuration\" (Ch12 p.117).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 12 p.117"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "cycle-voice-leading",
      "narrative": "When maj7 chords appear in cycle-of-fifths progressions, Carter prescribes tracking common tones held between adjacent chords while the remaining voices move by half step or whole step. He analyzes V7-to-I motion showing the tritone resolving inward and common tones maintained (Ch16 pp.155-157): \"If you think of harmonic movement as several moving voices rather than a glob of dots on a chord box grid, your arrangements and improvisations will be more airy, more interesting and less clunky.\"",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 16 pp.155-157"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "solo-arrangement-minimal-voicing",
      "narrative": "In solo fingerstyle arrangements, maj7 (and all chords) should be voiced as two or three notes so the melody can dominate. Carter states: \"Chords, for instance, are most often only two or three notes, and the melody is all-important\" (Ch18 p.169).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 18 p.169"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "harmonic-family-membership",
      "narrative": "Exactly one dominant seventh chord is diatonic to any major key: the V. Carter states: \"One dominant seventh V\" (Ch12 p.117). Its singularity in the key makes it the primary tension chord and the defining element of the II-V-I progression.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 12 p.117"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "II-V-I-shell-voicing",
      "narrative": "In the II-V-I recipe, the dominant seventh is voiced with just root and third. Carter specifies: \"dominant seventh defined with just root and third\" (Ch13 p.129). This minimal voicing communicates the dom7 function clearly while leaving textural space for the melody above.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement",
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.129"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tritone-resolution",
      "narrative": "The tritone interval embedded in the dom7 resolves inward by half step to the root and third of the target I chord. Carter illustrates: \"You can just feel the tug of the unresolved Bb and E (the tritone interval of a'5) being pulled by the A and F in the resolved F chord\" (Ch16 p.156).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 16 p.156"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tritone-half-step-movement-preference",
      "narrative": "In modern jazz, Carter prescribes the half-step movement of the dom7 tritone as the primary voice-leading mechanism. He writes: \"the half step movement of the tritone interval here on beats one and three is more common than root movement. This is especially true if you are playing with a bass player who covers roots and fifths\" (Ch17 p.159).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 17 p.159"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tritone-substitution",
      "narrative": "Any dom7 chord may be replaced by the dominant seventh built a tritone away, since both chords share the same tritone interval. Carter presents tritone substitution as a practical voicing swap in Ch7.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 7"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "intro-structural-requirement",
      "narrative": "Every introduction must conclude on a dom7 chord (V7 or substitute). Carter states: \"The last chord of the intro must be a V7 or V7 substitute in order to move into the first chord of the tune itself\" (Ch17 p.160).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:structural-devices"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 17 p.160"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tag-structural-requirement",
      "narrative": "In a tag ending, the final tonic chord is replaced by a dom7 (V7 or substitute) to restart harmonic motion. Carter specifies: \"the replacement of the usual final I chord -- E, here -- with a V7 (or other substitute) chord is required\" (Ch17 p.164).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:structural-devices"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 17 p.164"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "modulation-device",
      "narrative": "Modulation to a new key can be executed in as little as two beats by borrowing the V7 of the target key. Carter writes: \"You can modulate as fast as two beats...Use the last two beats to borrow V7 (Ab7) from the upcoming new key, Db\" (Ch17 p.165).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:structural-devices"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 17 p.165"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "fourths-tritones-voicing-ensemble-only",
      "narrative": "In ensemble settings with a bassist, the dom7 may be voiced as stacked alternating fourths and tritones with root and fifth omitted. Carter notes: \"There is no explicitly stated root or fifth of the chord. If you play in a group, the bassist will cover roots and fifths\" (Ch13 p.130).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading",
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.130"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "comping-density-policy",
      "narrative": "When comping behind a soloist, Carter prescribes filling the soloist's rests and thinning out when the soloist plays busily: \"play more when the soloist is not playing, and play less when he is\" (Ch14 p.135).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 14 p.135"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "harmonic-family-membership",
      "narrative": "Three minor seventh chords are diatonic to every major key: the II, III, and VI degrees. Carter states: \"Three minor sevenths II, III and VI\" (Ch12 p.117). The II is the most load-bearing as the gateway into the II-V-I progression.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 12 p.117"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "II-V-I-shell-voicing",
      "narrative": "In the II-V-I recipe, the minor seventh chord at II is voiced with root and seventh only. Carter specifies: \"a minor seventh defined by a root and seventh followed by the dominant seventh defined with just root and third\" (Ch13 p.129).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement",
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.129"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "root-seventh-alternation",
      "narrative": "As a self-accompaniment device, Carter prescribes alternating the root and seventh of a min7 chord in the bass. He notes: \"Keeping a strict pattern of root/seventh is not as important as long as the effect (low to high alternation) is there\" (Ch13 p.124).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.124"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "fourths-tritones-voicing-ensemble-only",
      "narrative": "Like the dom7, min7 chords may be voiced as stacked fourths and tritones omitting root and fifth in ensemble contexts. Carter's instruction: \"There is no explicitly stated root or fifth of the chord. If you play in a group, the bassist will cover roots and fifths\" (Ch13 p.130).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading",
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.130"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "harmonic-family-membership",
      "narrative": "Exactly one half-diminished seventh chord is diatonic to each major key: the VII degree. Carter states: \"One half-diminished seventh\" (Ch12 p.117).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 12 p.117"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "passing-substitution-function",
      "narrative": "The half-diminished seventh functions as a passing or substitution chord within the cycle-of-fifths voice-leading system. Its diatonic position at VII and its flattened fifth give it a naturally unstable quality suited for chromatic or diatonic passing motion between more stable chords.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 12 p.117"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "chromatic-passing-chord",
      "narrative": "The diminished seventh chord functions as a chromatic connector between adjacent diatonic chords. Carter's characterization is definitive: \"the diminished seventh chord is the in-between, the hamburger helper of\" passing chords (Ch13 p.121).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.121"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "ascending-chromatic-use",
      "narrative": "The dim7 is specifically deployed to create ascending chromatic harmonic movement -- inserted between two diatonic chords a step apart to produce smooth half-step-by-half-step bass motion. Carter demonstrates this ascending chromatic application in Ch13 p.121.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:cycle-voice-leading"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.121"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "aug7",
      "function_role": "altered-fifth-rule",
      "narrative": "When a chord symbol indicates an altered fifth, Carter prescribes a direct mechanical adjustment: raise the fifth a half step for #5, or lower it for b5. He states: \"If the chord is C7#5, raise the fifth a half step, and if it is C7b5, lower the fifth a half step\" (Ch7 p.77).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 7 p.77"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "thirds-harmonization",
      "narrative": "Melody notes are harmonized by adding a diatonic third below, with the interval quality determined by the chord symbol. Carter: \"Try putting thirds below each melody note. Follow the chord symbols to check for the correct third, and/or look at the TABLE OF THIRDS on the next page\" (Ch9 p.93).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 8 p.91"
        },
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.93"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "non-chord-tone-thirds-policy",
      "narrative": "When the diatonic third below a melody note is not a chord tone, Carter's rule is to let the ear decide: \"Non-chord tones make for the interesting spice in an otherwise dull chord-tone-only dish. It's your ear and your decision\" (Ch9 p.93).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.93"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "mandatory-third-when-ambiguous",
      "narrative": "When a melody note is ambiguous between major and minor, the accompaniment must supply the third. Carter states: \"The accompaniment needs the major or minor third to clarify the specific harmony of each measure\" (Ch7 p.83).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 7 p.83"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "thirds-bass-anchor-requirement",
      "narrative": "A melody harmonized only in thirds may sustain for a section but not an entire arrangement. Carter prescribes: \"Put the bass back in. Even a simple root/root and root/fifth bass can turn the melody with thirds into a full three-part arrangement\" (Ch9 p.93).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.93"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "sixths-key-note-on-top",
      "narrative": "When harmonizing in sixths, the key note must occupy the upper voice. Carter warns: \"If the key note were in the bottom position the ear would perceive the scale as being in a minor key rather than a major key\" (Ch11 p.108).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 11 p.108"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "tenths-independent-rhythm",
      "narrative": "Tenths below a melody note function as independent bass notes with their own rhythmic values. Carter states: \"The tenth does often function as a bass note, but only infrequently does it move parallel to the melodic rhythm. More often it has its own independent rhythm\" (Ch9 p.98).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.98"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "tenths-parallel-limitation",
      "narrative": "Parallel tenths should not run throughout an entire arrangement because doing so locks the bass rhythm into the melody rhythm. Carter: \"You probably would not use parallel tenths throughout the arrangement\" (Ch9 p.98).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "bill-carter:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar:solo-arrangement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.98"
        }
